Epistemic status and abstract

Epistemic status. This is a theoretical dissertation proposal. No humanoid has been built, no workstation has been purchased, and SI TEAM has executed no benchmark for this work. Every number below carries one of five tags:

· [V] vendor-documented figure.
· [C] closed-form calculation whose inputs are stated.
· [M] measurement made by a third party on their own system. It bounds what to expect; it is not evidence about our hardware.
· [S] reported by a secondary source and not checked at the primary. Treat as a lead, not as a fact.
· [T] target, hypothesis or acceptance threshold, to be settled by measurement.

Where a conclusion is stated, it follows from arithmetic on tagged quantities, not from an experiment. The discipline is the one of the workstation blueprint (28 October 2026): measure before investing, bound every queue, and keep fact, inference and hypothesis apart.

Abstract. Humanoid control in 2026 is built as stacked networks running at different speeds: a slow vision-language reasoner, a fast visuomotor policy emitting action chunks, and, in some systems, a still faster balance loop. These stacks are designed for a computer carried on the robot. The SI TEAM workstation (one RTX 5090, 32 GB, peak GPU TGP 575 W [V], wall draw about 955–1,075 W as the sum of GPU, CPU and PSU loss [C]) cannot be carried: against a 2.3 kWh pack [S] it would flatten the battery in 2.3 hours before the robot moved a limb. So "the workstation is the humanoid's brain" cannot mean on board.

This dissertation proposes split-latency cognition: the workstation is the deliberative cortex and the body carries a brainstem that never depends on it. We state five propositions: a continuity bound on chunk execution under link delay (T1); a policy-dependent stop-performance requirement (T2a), following Sun et al.'s formalization of emergency stopping as a policy-dependent stochastic reach-avoid condition; a certification-gap proposition (T2b), following Ding et al.'s localization of the uncertifiable residual at the robot-side reaction chain; a placement rule based on capture-region feasibility under the measured latency CDF (T3); and a capacity region for one GPU serving several robots under a bounded-queue controller with EDF scheduling within the robot-serving class (T4). We then give ten kill tests with pre-registered thresholds, a safety and authority model in which the learned stoppability estimator is telemetry only, and a gated roadmap that begins with a two-week tethered-rig latency measurement. The thesis is falsifiable: if the continuity or outage tests fail on a real link, the partition degrades to an on-board policy with the workstation as an advisory planner, and we state in advance what the smaller thesis asserts and how it would be refuted. No part of this document reports an experiment performed by SI TEAM.

