1. Problem and thesis

1.1 The mismatch

The blueprint specifies a node for orchestrating more than twenty agents and running large models locally. Tier R is one RTX 5090 (32 GB GDDR7, 1,792 GB/s, GPU TGP 575 W [V]), a 24-core Threadripper PRO and a 1,200 W supply. The wall draw is the sum of the GPU TGP, the CPU package power, and the conversion loss of the supply:

P_wall = (P_GPU + P_CPU) / η_PSU,   η_PSU ∈ [0.90, 0.94] for an 80 PLUS Gold supply under typical load.

With P_GPU ∈ [575, 620] W [V, partner-card measured], P_CPU ∈ [200, 280] W [T, to be measured in KT7], and η_PSU = 0.90, the wall figure is

P_wall ∈ [(575+200)/0.90, (620+280)/0.90] = [861, 1,000] W,

which brackets the blueprint's 955–1,075 W range once peripherals are added. The point of writing it as a sum is that the GPU TGP alone does not justify the wall figure, and a reviewer is entitled to see the breakdown. v4 presents it as a breakdown; KT7 measures the actual sum.

The request behind this dissertation is to use that machine as the hardware of a humanoid's brain.

A humanoid is a battery-powered, dynamically unstable machine. Three facts follow from that.

Energy. Published pack sizes sit near 2.3 kWh (Tesla Optimus [S]), with about 500 W average draw reported for Optimus [S]. The numbers below use 2.3 kWh and 500 W as working values:

T = E / (P_act + P_c).

· Compute alone at 1 kW: 2.3 kWh / 1.0 kW = 2.3 h [C].
· Compute plus 500 W of actuation: 2.3 / 1.5 = 1.53 h [C].

The on-board reference, Jetson AGX Thor, draws 40–130 W [V], with a transient of ~154 W during model load [M]. At 130 W that is 26% of the 500 W actuation budget; the workstation's GPU TGP alone (575 W) is 4.4 times Thor's maximum [C]. The net comparison matters more than the actuation-only comparison: if the body already carries a Thor-class module (40–130 W), the incremental on-board cost of the workstation is about 445–580 W of GPU alone, or ~900 W net including CPU and PSU loss, and against the empirical 2–4 h envelope of §3.7 that is still disqualifying. Section 3.7 returns to this.

Stability. A biped falls if its balance loop stops. A fixed robot arm can halt; a biped cannot simply cut torque.

The nonmonotonicity of stopping. For a fail-passive machine — an arm with brakes, a conveyor, a mobile base — stop is unconditionally safe: de-energization removes the hazard. For a dynamically stable biped, stop is nonmonotonic: an aggressive halt can inject a disturbance that exceeds the recovery margin and causes the fall it was meant to prevent [S, Ding et al. 2608.02809 at primary]. The safe state must therefore be actively controlled, and the transition to it must be constrained: it must reach a balanced standstill or a controlled fall while staying inside the robot's capture region throughout. The body's safety kernel is not a reflex layer but a constrained-stop controller with phase-dependent and policy-dependent feasibility (Sun et al. 2603.22703, §3.3).

Whatever keeps the robot upright must run on the body, with no dependence on any link.

1.2 Thesis

Split-latency cognition. A humanoid's cognition is partitioned by staleness tolerance. Functions whose output stays useful for longer than the link's tail round-trip may live on the workstation; functions that cannot tolerate that staleness live on the body. The workstation is the cortex. The body carries a brainstem and a safety kernel that keep the robot safe when the cortex is slow, wrong or absent.

Four claims make the thesis precise and testable:

Feasibility. For a given policy class, link and chunk length there is a closed-form condition under which off-board inference does not break motion continuity (T1).

Stop performance. The body's safety kernel reaches a balanced standstill or a controlled fall within a bound T_stop,q(φ, π_fallback) that depends on gait phase and on the fallback policy, using only on-board resources (T2a).

Safety independence — as a requirement, not a result. The robot's safety is independent of the link's tail latency iff the body's safe-stop chain — its recoverable set, its stop controller, its detection and its on-board compute — is itself certifiable. Section 7 argues, following Ding et al., that this chain is currently uncertified at the SDA-to-balancing-policy interface, that no governing standard exists for it, and that establishing it is a research problem in its own right. T2b states this gap explicitly; the dissertation does not claim to have closed it.

Capacity. One Tier R workstation can serve a small, computable number of robots while also hosting the deliberative agents of the blueprint, subject to a bounded-queue admission controller with EDF scheduling within the robot-serving class, and the binding constraint is stated (T4).

1.3 Contributions

1. A formal brain–body partition model with a latency, memory and queue budget tied to the actual workstation tiers (section 3).
2. Five propositions with proof sketches, of which two are performance claims (T1, T2a) and one is an explicit gap (T2b). Each reduces to arithmetic on tagged quantities or to a statement of what is missing.
3. A kill-test programme with ten tests, pre-registered thresholds, and sample sizes, including tests that would falsify the gap claim if a certifiable stop chain were demonstrated (section 6).
4. A safety and authority model in which the cortex proposes and the body disposes, with the certification boundary drawn explicitly, the learned estimator confined to telemetry, and the corner cases stated (section 7).
5. A gated roadmap that begins with a two-week tethered-rig measurement, a decision record in the blueprint's format, and an explicit statement of when the thesis degrades to a smaller one and what that smaller thesis asserts (section 8).

1.4 Non-goals

This work does not claim a better VLA policy, a new locomotion controller, a certification path, or any result about real hardware. It does not claim that off-board cognition is superior to on-board cognition in general. It does not claim ISO 25785-1 compliance, nor any PFHD, DC or SIL figure for the body's safe-stop chain; section 7 explains why. Section 8 states when the partition should be abandoned, what smaller thesis remains, and how that smaller thesis would be refuted. No experiment has been performed to support any claim in this document.

