Two lessons matter for this work. First, every successful stack separates a slow, semantic loop from a fast, motor loop, and the fast loop emits a chunk of future actions rather than one action. A chunk is a buffer of autonomy. Second, where the boundary between the layers should sit is an empirical question. Libra-VLA (arXiv:2604.24921, ACL 2026) reports an inverted-U in performance against action-decomposition granularity [S]; we treat the boundary as a parameter to be measured, not a fixed design.

A third lesson: action chunking is not sufficient for contact-rich tasks. A 2026 review of action-chunking methods states that contact-rich manipulation and locomotion require reactive feedback within chunks [S]. RTC and its relatives address between-chunk consistency; they do not enforce contact feasibility inside a chunk. The 2026 learned-corrector literature (ACPPO-Corr, FA-RDP, TacForcing, PACE [S]) reduces the frequency of infeasible chunks but does not certify their absence. B2's contact-phase barrier is therefore positioned as the deterministic, closed-form safety filter under learned correctors — not as the only response to the within-chunk gap (§4.1, KT8).

2.2 Running a policy through a delay: Real-Time Chunking and FreeBar

Real-Time Chunking (RTC; Black, Galliker, Levine; Physical Intelligence) treats inference delay as a first-class constraint. With chunk length H, execution horizon s and inference delay d = ⌊δ/Δt⌋ steps, it requires

d ≤ s ≤ H − d,

and inpaints the next chunk so that it stays consistent with the actions already committed. Reported measurements [M]:

· Remote inference on an RTX 4090 over a wired LAN: model time 97 ms with RTC (76 ms for vanilla π0.5); network 6.89 ms (non-mobile) and 21.2 ms (mobile); totals 108.76 ms and 138.98 ms.
· Robust to +100 ms and +200 ms of injected delay, while synchronous execution degraded roughly linearly.

FreeBar (arXiv:2610.14997, "Barrier-Constrained Guidance For Real-time Asynchronous VLA Execution," CoRL 2026) provides a training-free, CBF-inspired consistency constraint between consecutive chunks, solved in closed form. It reports >45% lower inference latency than RTC and 10–15% higher success under asynchronous execution [primary]. For this proposal FreeBar matters in two ways: it weakens the assumption that RTC is the only viable off-board chunker, and it gives B2 a natural place to attach a barrier constraint rather than only an error tube.

Neither RTC nor FreeBar enforces foot placement, contact timing or capture-point feasibility. Both are between-chunk methods. The within-chunk contact problem has a learned-corrector literature (§2.1); what it does not yet have is a certifiable within-chunk check. That is what K7 in §4.2 and KT8 in §6.2 provide.

2.3 Remote or speculative control under network delay

Speculative Policy Orchestration (SPO, arXiv:2603.19418) has a cloud world model stream waypoints, an edge verifier enforce an error tube, and an AIMD rule adapt the speculation horizon K, with K_max·Δt ≥ τ_net. Torque servoing stays local. It was tested only in simulation, with a 150 ± 30 ms round trip [M, simulation]. Its "zero-velocity hold" fallback is unsafe for a biped and must be replaced by an actively controlled stop (§7). The paper notes that 5G and Wi-Fi handovers can add delays of hundreds of milliseconds to seconds, which is the failure mode KT1 and KT9 target.

Earlier work on 5G remote whole-body control kept a local linear controller on the body and offloaded only the higher level (Zhu et al., 2020) [S]. The pattern recurs: the cortex is remote, the reflex is local.

