The workstation comparison, corrected. v2 gave a roofline bound of 102 tok/s for a 32B 4-bit model on the 5090 (17.5 GB weights, 1,792 GB/s). That bound is bandwidth-only. Deployed single-stream decode on a 5090 is about 32 tok/s for a 32B Q4 model, and aggregate batched throughput is about 900 tok/s [M]. The single-stream ratio to Thor (~15.6 tok/s) is therefore roughly 2×, not 6.5×. The 6.5× bandwidth ratio (1,792 vs 273 GB/s [C]) is real but is realized in prefill and batched decode, not single-stream decode.

Consequence. The argument for placing the critic on the workstation is not single-stream decode. It is (a) batched prefill and batched decode, which let one 5090 serve the critic for several robots at once; (b) prefill of long contexts, which is bandwidth-bound and does benefit from HBM; (c) co-location of the critic with the robot-serving VLA on the same card (§3.5). A reviewer who reads only the single-stream number will conclude the workstation is 2× Thor. The dissertation's answer is: the workload is batched and prefill-heavy, not single-stream.

2.5 Safety standards, the fail-passive gap, and the active safe state

ISO/CD 25785-1 covers dynamically stable industrial mobile robots ("actively controlled stability") and is at Committee Draft stage (30.60, close of comment; CD registered 8 May 2026, comment period closed 8 July 2026) [V]. Its abstract covers actively controlled stability and industrial environments; non-industrial use is out of scope even after publication. It is not yet a standard, and a 2026 industry review notes that humanoid robots may reach customers before it is finished [S].

ISO 13482 covers personal-care robots; ISO/IEC TS 22440 addresses AI and functional safety [S].

The fail-passive gap (primary). Ding et al., arXiv:2608.02809 (submitted 3 August 2026), is a feasibility study of safe stopping for dynamically stable humanoids. It confirms that the safe state of a legged robot is actively controlled, violating the fail-passive assumption of ISO 13849-1 and EN 60204-1, and it locates the uncertifiable residual at the robot-side reaction chain — specifically at the interface between the safety-rated detection-and-stopping layer and the balancing policy. It states that the Unitree G1's onboard compute is not safety-rated hardware, and that the external chain achieves PL e while the humanoid reaction chain establishes no PFHD, hence no PL. Three consequences follow:

1. The safe state is a constrained stop: minimize stopping time and distance while staying inside the capturable region. This is a reach-avoid problem, not a bang-bang halt.
2. Feasibility is phase-dependent: short in double support, bounded below in single support by the time needed to place the current step and reach a capture point.
3. The trade-off is nonmonotonic: an overly aggressive halt can inject a disturbance that exceeds the recovery margin and cause the fall it was meant to prevent.

Safe-Stop (primary). Long et al., arXiv:2609.02358, casts emergency stopping as a reach-avoid problem: a learned stop policy paired with two complementary estimators, committed only when they agree, with handoff to a damping fallback otherwise. The architecture is the closest existing template for what B0 must do. Its damping fallback is the deterministic corner-case policy that v4 adopts (§7.2).

PRISM (primary). Sun et al., "Learning Safe-Stoppability Monitors for Humanoid Robots," arXiv:2603.22703 (IROS 2026), formalizes emergency stopping as a policy-dependent stochastic reach-avoid condition under a fixed fallback policy, and uses importance sampling to reduce false-safe predictions. This is the formal reason T2a must depend on the fallback policy and not on gait phase alone (§3.3), and it is the reason KT5a is split into a false-safe-rate test and a fallback-execution test (§6.2).

Other safety layers. NVIDIA Halos for Robotics and IGX Thor, with a SIL 3-capable safety island, were announced on 22 June 2026 [S, vendor claim]. Control-barrier-function filters for learned humanoid policies (CBF-RL, SHIELD, ConstrainedMimic) show how a certified layer can sit under an unverified policy [S]. These are relevant to B2, not to B0's certification.

2.6 Capture region and the balance-placement literature

The placement criterion in §3.4 rests on two standard objects [S]:

· The capture point: the point on the ground such that, if the robot places its foot there and transfers support, it comes to a stop.
· The capture region ℛ_cap: the set of states from which the robot can reach a capture point before its motion exceeds the recoverable margin.

Pratt et al. (2006) states that computing the capture region for a humanoid in general is very difficult, but gives exact solutions for the linear inverted pendulum model (LIPM) with a flywheel. The criterion below therefore uses three approximations, with the gap between them reported as a first-class quantity rather than hidden:

· ℛ_cap^LIPM: analytic, conservative, closed-form for the LIPM with a flywheel.
· ℛ_cap^N: simulator N-step Monte Carlo capturable set.
· ℛ_cap^HW: hardware fall indicator, binary, from KT5a2.

The approximation gap is δ_cap = |ℛ_cap^LIPM Δ ℛ_cap^N| / |ℛ_cap^LIPM ∪ ℛ_cap^N|, reported alongside KT6. A placement decision that is robust across all three is treated as confirmed; one that holds only under ℛ_cap^LIPM is treated as conservative but unresolved.

2.7 Power systems for humanoids

The 2.3 kWh / 500 W figures in §1.1 are working values. A 2026 review of humanoid power systems adds two constraints [S]:

· Current platforms achieve only two to four hours of continuous operation, not the 4.6 h the average-draw model predicts at zero compute.
· Humanoid discharge is pulsed: hip and knee peak currents exceed sustained averages by three to five times. A pack sized on average draw will brown out under peak actuation unless oversized.

This matters for §3.7: the simple T = E/(P_act + P_c) model overstates usable endurance, and the sensitivity envelope must be stated. The link is not free either: P_link ∈ [15, 30] W [T] covers radio, additional sensing, and the on-board fallback B1.

2.8 Gap

No source we found places a mains-powered, multi-GPU workstation as the deliberative layer of a humanoid with an explicit brain–body contract covering delay, outage and capacity. The published designs assume the policy is on board (Thor, Orin) or demonstrate remote inference only for manipulation (RTC, FreeBar) or in simulation (SPO). The safe-stop literature names the fail-passive gap but does not connect it to a split-latency architecture. The within-chunk correction literature improves learned policies but does not supply a certifiable within-chunk check. This work fills the gap on paper, and section 6 says how to test it — and, in T2b, what it explicitly leaves open.

