3. Formal model

3.1 Loops and periods

Loop Role Period Where
ℓ₀ safety kernel, balance reflex, constrained stop ~1 ms body
ℓ₁ motor policy emitting chunks 5–20 ms steps, chunk every 0.1–1 s body or cortex (decided by T1, T3)
ℓ₂ semantic reasoner, critic, planner 0.1–10 s cortex
ℓ₃ long-horizon agents, memory, task management seconds to hours cortex

Each loop has a staleness tolerance τ: the age beyond which its input or output is no longer safe to use.

3.2 Latency budget

For an off-board loop, the end-to-end latency of one action chunk is a random variable:

L = t_sense + t_↑ + t_queue + t_infer + t_↓ + t_apply,

where t_↑ and t_↓ are the uplink and downlink delays, t_queue is waiting time at the GPU, and t_infer is service time. Let L_q denote the q-quantile of L. A chunk has H steps of duration Δt, so it provides an autonomy window

A = H·Δt.

The inference delay in steps is d = ⌈L_q/Δt⌉ (RTC uses a floor; we round up for a conservative design).

Instances [M]: for π0.5 with H = 50 and Δt = 20 ms, A = 1,000 ms; the RTC totals of 108.76 and 138.98 ms give d = 5 and 6 steps under RTC's floor rule. For GR00T N1, H = 16 at 120 Hz gives A = 133 ms, so the same rule allows at most 67 ms of loop latency.

3.3 Policy-dependent stop

The LIPM growth time is retained as intuition only. For a linear inverted pendulum with CoM height z, the unstable pole is p = √(g/z). With z = 0.9 m, p = 3.30 s⁻¹ and 1/p ≈ 303 ms [C]. Open-loop, the unstable mode grows by a factor M in

t = ln(M)/p.

M t
1.2 55 ms
1.5 123 ms
2.0 210 ms

These are [C] for the LIPM and say nothing about a real robot's margins. They are used only as an order-of-magnitude sanity bound, not as a placement criterion.

Following Sun et al. (2603.22703), the v4 model replaces the scalar T_safe with a policy-dependent constrained stop. Let φ be the gait phase (double support, single support left/right, flight if applicable), π_fb a fixed fallback policy, and ℛ_cap(φ) the capture region at that phase. A stop is feasible at (φ, π_fb) if π_fb reaches a balanced standstill or a controlled fall while keeping every intermediate state inside ℛ_cap. Define

T_stop(φ, π_fb) = minimum feasible stop time at phase φ under fallback policy π_fb, subject to ℛ_cap(φ) at every instant.

The key structural facts are:

· T_stop is not minimized by maximizing deceleration. The minimum-time stop is the solution of a reach-avoid problem, not a bang-bang halt.
· T_stop is phase-dependent: short in double support, bounded below in single support.
· T_stop is policy-dependent: changing π_fb changes T_stop even at fixed φ. This is the PRISM formalization, and it is why KT5a is split.
· T_stop is a random variable under actuation and sensing noise, so the design quantity is a quantile T_stop,q(φ, π_fb), not a mean.

The separation-distance analogue must use the worst-case single-support T_stop at the chosen fallback, matching Ding et al.'s recommendation.

3.4 Placement criterion: capture-region feasibility under the latency CDF

Let F_L be the measured latency CDF from KT1. For a balance-related function f with tolerance τ_f, f may be cortex-resident only if, under F_L,

Pr[∃ t ∈ [0, L_f] : state(t) ∉ ℛ_cap(φ(t))] ≤ ε_cap,

and its q-quantile loop latency satisfies L_{1−ε_f}(f) ≤ τ_f. Otherwise it is body-resident.

The criterion is evaluated under all three capture-region approximations of §2.6. A function is placed on the cortex only if the inequality holds under ℛ_cap^LIPM (conservative) and is not contradicted by ℛ_cap^N. The gap δ_cap is reported in KT6.

The LIPM table in §3.3 remains as intuition: a loop needing correction within tens of milliseconds cannot sit behind a 100–140 ms link, which already places reflex balance on the body. It does not settle the boundary for slower balance functions; the capture-region criterion does.

