Throughput framing. Deployed single-stream decode for a 32B Q4 model on the 5090 is about 32 tok/s; aggregate under batching is about 900 tok/s [M]. The decision record sizes the critic as follows:

· Single-robot, latency-sensitive review: a 7B–14B critic at 4-bit.
· Multi-robot, batched review: the 32B critic, whose aggregate throughput under batching is the relevant quantity.

This is the honest reading of the 5090's advantage: batched and prefill-heavy work, not single-stream decode.

3.6 Queueing, admission, and EDF within the VLA class

Why M/D/1 is optimistic. Independent Poisson arrivals are a reasonable floor, but three effects push the real tail above it: (a) multiple robots can synchronize chunk releases; (b) GPU batching makes service time depend on the batch; (c) shared HBM contention under co-residency couples the two workloads. The M/D/1 table is retained as a first bound; KT4 tests against synchronized and bursty traffic.

The controller. W4 implements:

· A hard queue bound Q_max per worker, swept in KT4 and set from the knee of the p99-versus-Q_max curve, not fixed at [8, 12] without source.
· A per-robot deadline D_i = ⌊H/2⌋Δt − t_rest,i, where t_rest,i is the non-queue portion of robot i's loop latency measured in KT1.
· Admission before scheduling. A request that would make |Q| > Q_max is refused (K5) at admission; it does not age in the queue.
· EDF within the VLA class. Among admitted VLA requests, the smallest D_i runs first. EDF is optimal for single-server deadline feasibility under the classical assumptions; the queue bound handles the infeasible case.
· Class priority between VLA and critic. The robot-serving VLA preempts the critic at the class boundary. KT3 measures critic starvation: the fraction of time the critic receives zero service under sustained VLA load. A minimum critic rate (e.g. 1 Hz) is either guaranteed by a reserved slot or explicitly abandoned as best-effort.
· Adaptive tightening. If observed p99 approaches the deadline, Q_max is reduced until the tail returns to budget.
· Logging. Every admission, refusal, preemption and service time is logged with both clocks (K6).

The bound. For an M/D/1 queue with service time S and N robots each requesting a chunk every sΔt seconds:

λ = N/(sΔt),  ρ = λS,  W_q = ρS/(2(1−ρ)).

With S = 27.9 ms [M, RTX Pro 6000; the 5090's value is [T], measured in KT2] and Δt = 20 ms:

s Chunk period N = 8: ρ N = 8: W_q Largest N with ρ < 1
25 500 ms 0.45 11.2 ms 17
16 320 ms 0.70 32.2 ms 11
10 200 ms 1.12 saturated 7

All [C]. At s = 16, N = 12 gives ρ = 1.05 and the queue grows without bound. At N = 17 the mean wait is ~257 ms, half the 500 ms continuity budget before network and tails; the usable N is far below the stability bound.

