4. Architecture

4.1 Two machines, one contract

Body (brainstem).

· B0, safety kernel. A small, deterministic program on a real-time core. Reads proprioception and a heartbeat, enforces joint, torque and stability limits, owns the constrained-stop controller of §3.3, and owns the deterministic damping fallback of §7.2. No learned component is on the certification path in any state. The learned stoppability estimator is telemetry only — it is logged, it is used for post-hoc analysis, and it does not decide anything, including in the corner case.
· B1, motor policy. A fast visuomotor policy that turns observations and a chunk into joint targets. In the baseline it consumes chunks from the cortex. In the fallback it runs a smaller local policy on the on-board computer — a Thor-class module running a quantised GR00T-class model at 39.9 ms / 25.1 Hz (TensorRT mixed NVFP4, JetPack 7.2, MAXN, LIBERO, 4 steps, 1 camera) [M], with 23.7–29.9 ms (42/33 Hz) as the optimized ceiling. The named recipe is: NVFP4 weights, FP4 KV where validated, CUDA graphs, K/V hoisting.
· B2, chunk buffer and verifier. Holds the current chunk, blends with the committed prefix (RTC- or FreeBar-style), checks every chunk against an error tube, and checks every chunk against the capture region for the current and predicted gait phase. B2 is the deterministic, closed-form safety filter under learned correctors: it does not replace ACPPO-Corr, FA-RDP, TacForcing or PACE, and it does not assume them. It checks their output the way a runtime monitor checks any policy.

Workstation (cortex).

· W1, deliberative VLM or VLA. The slow reasoner and, when T1 permits, the chunk generator for several robots.
· W2, critic. A 7B–14B critic for single-robot, latency-sensitive review, or the 32B dense model at 4-bit for multi-robot batched review. Both sized against deployed throughput (§3.5). The critic never emits torques.
· W3, agents and memory. The blueprint's agent orchestrator, task queue, result registry and long-horizon memory.
· W4, link manager and admission controller. Heartbeat, clock sync, bounded queues, per-robot deadlines, EDF within the VLA class, class priority, refusal semantics, critic-starvation metric, logging.

4.2 The contract

# Obligation Held by
K1 Every chunk carries a sequence number, a timestamp, a horizon H and a validity deadline. An expired chunk is never executed. Cortex stamps, body checks
K2 The body never waits for the cortex to stay upright. Loss of every chunk leaves the body in a state from which B0 can reach a constrained stop. Body
K3 The cortex never has actuation authority. It proposes; B2 and B0 dispose. Body
K4 The link is monitored by a heartbeat with a known detection time T_det. Both
K5 The cortex bounds its own queue and returns an explicit refusal when it cannot meet a deadline. Cortex
K6 Every chunk, refusal, timeout and safe-state entry is logged with both clocks. Both
K7 Every admitted chunk is certified against the capture region for the current and predicted gait phase; a chunk that would leave ℛ_cap is refused, and B0 is notified with the predicted exit phase. B2

