5. Propositions and proof sketches

These are conditional statements. T2b is a gap claim. None is an experimental result.

5.1 T1 — Continuity bound

Assumptions. RTC (or FreeBar) constraint: chunk length H steps of Δt, execution horizon s, inference delay d steps, with d ≤ s ≤ H − d.

Statement. A feasible s exists if and only if H ≥ 2d, equivalently

L_q ≤ ⌊H/2⌋Δt = A/2.

Proof sketch. The interval [d, H−d] is non-empty iff d ≤ H−d. For integer d, ⌈x⌉ ≤ n iff x ≤ n. □

Instances [C on M inputs]. π0.5 (H = 50, Δt = 20 ms): L_q ≤ 500 ms; measured RTC totals 108.76 and 138.98 ms leave margins 4.6 and 3.6. GR00T N1 (H = 16 at 120 Hz): L_q ≤ 66.7 ms.

Corollary 1.1 (generation rate). T1 requires A/L ≥ 2. Reported Cosmos 3 Edge [S]: 1.40 on T5000, 0.97 on T4000, 0.81 on emulated T3000, 0.41 on T2000. Under the RTC constraint, none qualifies; below 1.0 the policy cannot keep pace with its own output. Whether the 5090 clears A/L ≥ 2 for such a policy is unmeasured [T] and is the cheapest falsification in the programme (KT2).

Corollary 1.2 (violation probability). The per-chunk probability of breaking continuity is 1 − F(A/2), estimated in KT1.

Corollary 1.3 (contact feasibility is not covered by T1). T1 guarantees between-chunk consistency; it says nothing about contact feasibility within a chunk. This is why K7 exists and why KT8 tests it. The 2026 learned correctors reduce the frequency of such chunks; they do not certify their absence, which is why B2's check remains deterministic.

5.2 T2a — Policy-dependent stop performance

Assumptions.

(a) The body holds a certified buffer of length A_b, with A_b ≥ T_det (detection occurs before the buffer runs out), where T_det is the heartbeat-detection time distributed per KT9.
(b) B0's constrained-stop controller has a measured phase- and policy-dependent stop time T_stop,q(φ, π_fb) at quantile q.
(c) The capture region ℛ_cap(φ) is defined and K7 is enforced on every admitted chunk.
(d) When B2 refuses a chunk and the constrained stop is infeasible, B0 executes the damping fallback deterministically, without consulting the estimator.

Statement. For any link behaviour, including total loss at an arbitrary instant, the body reaches a balanced standstill or a controlled fall within

T_det + T_stop,q(φ_last, π_fb),

where φ_last is the gait phase at the last valid heartbeat, and every intermediate state stays inside ℛ_cap(φ(t)) with probability at least 1 − ε_cap.

Proof sketch. By (c) the robot is in ℛ_cap at detection. By (a) the buffer has not run out. By (b) B0 executes the constrained stop, whose bound is what (b) asserts. By (d) the corner case is handled deterministically. The probability clause comes from the quantile in (b) and the K7 enforcement in (c). □

What it does and does not say. T2a is a performance proposition, not a safety certification. It does not say the body is safe; that is T2b's job, and T2b is a gap.

Corollary 2a.1 (worst-case phase and policy govern). Any separation-distance requirement must use max_φ T_stop,q(φ, π_fb) at the chosen π_fb, which in practice is the worst-case single-support value.

Corollary 2a.2 (nonmonotonicity). T_stop is not minimized by maximizing deceleration; the minimum-time stop is the reach-avoid solution.

Corollary 2a.3 (policy dependence). Changing π_fb changes T_stop at fixed φ. This is the PRISM formalization. It is why KT5a is split: the estimator's job is to predict stoppability under a fixed π_fb, and the fallback's job is to execute. A single trial count over both conflates two failure modes.

5.3 T2b — The certification gap

Statement. There is no governing standard, and no established PFHD, DC or fault-behaviour argument, for a humanoid's active safe-stop chain. In particular: (i) ISO/CD 25785-1 is at Committee Draft and does not yet specify the chain; (ii) the interface between the safety-rated detection-and-stopping layer and the balancing policy has no published PFHD or DC (Ding et al., primary); (iii) current on-board compute is not safety-rated hardware (Ding et al., primary); (iv) a learned stoppability estimator, even if it performs well, does not by itself furnish a certifiable argument.

Argument. Absence-of-standard claim supported by Ding et al. and by the ISO status page. It is falsifiable: refuted by a published, standard-compliant PFHD/DC argument for a humanoid active safe-stop chain, or by ISO 25785-1 reaching a stage that specifies the chain. KT5b's pass criterion is designed so that such a demonstration would move T2b from "gap" to "requirement satisfied" without changing the rest of the thesis.

Consequence. "Safety independent of the link" is a design goal that T2a supports at the performance level and T2b leaves open at the certification level. The dissertation must state this and must not claim ISO 25785-1 compliance, SIL, PFHD or DC for the body.

5.4 T3 — Placement rule

Statement. A function f with staleness tolerance τ_f and tolerable capture-region-exit probability ε_cap may be cortex-resident only if, under F_L,

Pr[∃ t ∈ [0, L_f] : state(t) ∉ ℛ_cap(φ(t))] ≤ ε_cap

and L_{1−ε_f}(f) ≤ τ_f. Otherwise body-resident.

Proof sketch. Failure of the first inequality means the robot leaves the recoverable set with probability above ε_cap before f's output arrives, which is infeasibility for a balance-related function. The second inequality is v1's T3 for non-balance functions. □

Corollary 3.1 (reflex balance). At z = 0.9 m the LIPM unstable mode grows by 20% in 55 ms [C]. The RTC loop alone totals 108.76–138.98 ms [M]. Reflex-level balance is body-resident, agreeing with the three-speed stacks (1 kHz System 0 in Helix 02 [S]). Intuition, not the formal criterion.

Corollary 3.2 (tails cannot be certified by sampling). For a hazard budget of one latency-induced fault per 1,000 h at 2 Hz, ε = 1/(2·3,600·1,000) = 1.39×10⁻⁷ [C]. Rule of three: 3/ε ≈ 2.16×10⁷ chunks, or 3,000 h of traffic [C]. Safety-grade latency tails cannot be established by measurement within a normal project. This is why T3 sets ε_f at a performance level (e.g. 10⁻³, with exceedance triggering graceful degradation), and why the hazard is carried in principle by T2a and in practice by the open problem in T2b.

5.5 T4 — Capacity region under EDF

Statement. One workstation GPU with service time S can serve N robots with horizon s only if all five hold:

(i) N < sΔt/S,
(ii) t_queue,q + t_rest ≤ ⌊H/2⌋Δt,
(iii) M_VRAM ≤ 0.90 M_GPU,
(iv) Q_max is never exceeded under the arrival process,
(v) EDF within the VLA class meets D_i whenever Q_max is respected and no synchronized burst exceeds one service quantum.

(i) is stability, (ii) is T1 with the queue term broken out, (iii) is the memory budget, (iv) is the admission invariant, (v) is the EDF guarantee. (iv) and (v) are what v1 and v2 lacked: without (iv) a synchronized burst can drive the queue past the deadline even when ρ < 1; without (v) the deadline-miss rate is a measured outcome rather than a design consequence.

Proof sketch. (i) is ρ < 1. (ii) splits L_q. (iii) is the vLLM budget. (iv) is the refusal rule: any arrival that would make |Q| > Q_max is refused, so |Q| ≤ Q_max is an invariant. (v) is classical EDF optimality, with refusal handling the infeasible case. □

Worked example [C, S assumed from RTX Pro 6000, [T] on the 5090]. S = 27.9 ms, s = 25, Δt = 20 ms: (i) gives N ≤ 17, but N = 17 means ρ = 0.95 and mean wait ~257 ms, half the continuity budget before network and tails. At N = 8, ρ = 0.45 and W_q = 11.2 ms. Co-residency with the 32B critic leaves a KV pool of 2.3 GB (§3.5).

Corollary 4.1. Condition (iii) makes the workstation a scarce shared resource; raising N or critic context reduces the other. Capacity is cumulative, not pooled.

Corollary 4.2 (priority and starvation). VLA-over-critic priority keeps (ii) satisfiable when the workloads compete, but it can starve the critic. KT3 measures the starvation fraction; a reserved minimum critic rate is either guaranteed or explicitly abandoned as best-effort.

