6.3 What would falsify the thesis

1. KT1 fails on every available link for the chunk policy the task needs.
2. KT2 shows A/L < 2 on the 5090 for the target policy — the cheapest and most decisive falsification, available before any robot is purchased.
3. KT5a2 or KT8 fails after reasonable redesign: the fallback cannot execute from estimator-approved states, or the contact-phase barrier cannot separate feasible from infeasible chunks even with a learned corrector.
4. KT9 shows T_det unbounded: T2a's bound is meaningless and the heartbeat protocol must be redesigned before any safety claim is attempted.
5. Phase 4 shows the on-board fallback (baselined against 39.9 ms / 25.1 Hz, not the 93.8 ms model card) matches the split within the margin.

In case 5, the workstation remains useful for ℓ₂ and ℓ₃. The smaller thesis asserts:

Smaller thesis (advisory cortex). When the on-board fallback matches the split on motor-level task success and smoothness, the workstation still reduces time-to-task-completion by ≥ X% through better plan critique, memory retrieval and multi-robot scheduling, even though it never emits a chunk. Refuted if, in the phase-4 comparison, the advisory-only cortex does not improve time-to-completion by the pre-registered margin, or if the improvement is explained entirely by the fallback's own reasoning.

The threshold X is [T]. The smaller thesis is falsifiable in its own right.

T2b is not a falsifier; it is a scope limit. A future demonstration of a certifiable active safe-stop chain would close T2b without changing anything else.

