7. Safety and authority

7.1 Authority model

· Cortex proposes. W1 generates chunks; W2 critiques plans; W3 orchestrates. None emits torques or any signal that reaches an actuator without passing through B2 and B0.
· B2 disposes of chunks. Expiry (K1), error tube (SPO-style), capture region (K7). A chunk that fails any check is refused and B0 is notified.
· B0 disposes of motion. Enforces joint, torque and stability limits, runs the constrained-stop controller, and can initiate a stop without any input from B2 or the cortex.
· B0 is the last authority. If B2 and the cortex disagree, B0's limits win. If the link is lost, B0 alone is sufficient.

7.2 The safe state and the corner case

The safe state is not torque-off and not a frozen posture. It is one of:

· a balanced standstill inside ℛ_cap, reached by the constrained-stop controller, or
· a controlled fall, entered from a state where the constrained stop is infeasible, with the fall policy selected before the state leaves ℛ_cap.

The corner case. There is a state in which B2 refuses a chunk and the constrained stop is infeasible. B0 carries a pre-registered capture-region margin threshold m_d, calibrated in KT5a2. When B2 refuses and the measured margin is below m_d, B0 executes the damping fallback deterministically, without consulting the estimator. The estimator is telemetry only. This is the Safe-Stop fallback (Long et al., arXiv:2609.02358) in deterministic form, and it is why T2a's assumption (d) exists.

7.3 Certification boundary

1. B0's deterministic limits, the ℛ_cap check, and the damping fallback. In principle certifiable against a standard, but no standard yet specifies the active safe-stop chain (T2b, Ding et al.). The structural claim lives here: B0's ℛ_cap check is an invariant, verified by construction and by KT5a2's separate check, not by a trial count.
2. The constrained-stop controller and the stoppability estimator. Performance-validated (KT5a1, KT5a2, KT5b, KT5c) and, for the estimator, not certifiable by current methods.
3. The cortex and the link. Outside the safety argument by construction.

The boundary between (1) and (2) is the fail-passive gap. The contribution is to draw it explicitly, to state that (1) is uncertified because no standard specifies it, to make the corner case deterministic, and to give a test programme that validates (2) at the performance level while leaving (1) open.

7.4 What is not claimed

The proposal does not claim ISO 25785-1 compliance; any PFHD, DC or SIL figure for B0, B2, the constrained-stop controller or the estimator; that KT5a1, KT5a2, KT5b or KT5c, passing at 299 trials, constitutes a safety certification; or that the estimator is certifiable. The structural claim rests on B0's deterministic check, not on any trial count. It does claim that the architecture keeps the cortex and the link outside the safety argument, that the corner case is deterministic, and that this is testable (KT1, KT2, KT5a1, KT5a2, KT5b, KT5c, KT8, KT9, KT10) even though it is not yet certifiable.

