8. Roadmap and abandonment criteria

8.1 Gated phases

· Phase 0.5 — tethered-rig latency measurement. Two weeks. Measure (a) the latency CDF of one real Wi-Fi 6/6E link and one wired link under representative chunk traffic; (b) the service time S and the real-time factor A/L of a GR00T-class and a π0.5-class model on the 5090 at FP8 and NVFP4 with the TensorRT recipe. Deliverable: the first falsifiable data points for T1 and T4. Gate: L_p99 and S and A/L are recorded and the T1 margin A/(2L_p99) is computed. If A/L < 2 on every link and every policy, the thesis degrades to the smaller thesis before the burn-in begins.
· Phase 0 — platform and burn-in. Blueprint burn-in; KT7 power and thermal with the breakdown. Gate: pass KT7 before any inference benchmark.
· Phase 1 — single-robot link and latency. KT1, KT2, KT6 in simulation and on the tethered rig. Gate: KT1 p99.9 ≤ A/2 for at least one link and one policy class; KT2 A/L ≥ 2.
· Phase 2 — safety validation. KT5a1, KT5a2, KT5b, KT5c, KT8, KT9, KT10. Gate: KT9 produces a bounded T_det; KT5a1 false-safe rate within envelope; KT5a2 passes structurally (B0's deterministic check) and at performance; KT5b passes with T_det drawn from KT9; KT5c does not reveal a new failure mode; KT8 passes with a learned corrector in the loop; KT10 residual is stated. No cortex-resident chunk generation proceeds past this gate until KT5a2 and KT8 pass. KT5b alone is insufficient.
· Phase 3 — multi-robot capacity. KT3, KT4 with Q_max swept. Gate: KT4 passes under both Poisson and bursty arrival with EDF; N_max set from the measured curve; critic starvation stated.
· Phase 4 — end-to-end comparison. On-board fallback (against 39.9 ms / 25.1 Hz, with 23.7–29.9 ms as ceiling) versus split architecture on task success, smoothness and safety-event rate, with a pre-registered margin. Gate: split is better by the margin, or the thesis degrades to the smaller thesis whose own falsifiable claim is stated in §6.3.

8.2 Abandonment criteria

The split-latency architecture is abandoned, for a given robot and link, if:

· Phase 0.5 or Phase 1 fails on every available link, or A/L < 2 on the 5090 (KT1, KT2).
· Phase 2 fails after reasonable redesign (KT5a2 structural, KT8 with a learned corrector, or KT9 unbounded T_det).
· Phase 4 shows the fallback — baselined against 39.9 ms, not 93.8 ms — matches the split within the margin.

In the first two cases the architecture is not viable for that robot. In the third, the smaller thesis of §6.3 applies, with its own refutation condition.

