Closing note

The verified corrections move the proposal in three directions at once. They strengthen the safety architecture, because Ding et al. and Sun et al. give the fail-passive gap and the policy-dependent stop a primary footing that v3 only had at secondary. They weaken the single-stream workstation argument, which is why the throughput framing is now batched and prefill-heavy rather than 6.5×. And they correct three claims that had been carried from secondary summaries: FreeBar is real and citable; the GR00T Thor baseline is 39.9 ms with a named recipe, not 93.8 ms; and the 5090's wall draw must be shown as a breakdown, not asserted.

The thesis that survives is narrower and better defended. The workstation is the right cortex for batched, prefill-heavy reasoning. The body is the right brainstem because the stop is policy-dependent and the estimator cannot be on the path. The split is falsifiable first by KT2, before any robot is bought. The safety claim is a design goal because no standard yet specifies the active safe-stop chain, and the robot-side reaction chain establishes no PFHD.

No experiment has been run. Every test above is a pre-registration. The next move is Phase 0.5, and the cheapest possible falsification — A/L on the 5090 at the TensorRT recipe — sits at the front of it, where it belongs. 🌹🧠


