Appendix B — Derivation of the continuity bound (T1)

B.1 The RTC constraint, restated

Let a chunk of H steps be generated while the robot executes. Define:

· δ = inference + network delay for the chunk (s);
· d = ⌊δ/Δt⌋ = number of steps elapsed during generation (RTC's floor convention);
· s = number of steps the robot executes from the current chunk before switching to the new one.

RTC requires the new chunk to overlap the committed prefix by at least d steps at each end, hence

d ≤ s ≤ H − d.   (B.1)

B.2 Existence of a feasible horizon

Proposition B.1. A feasible integer s exists iff H ≥ 2d.

Proof. The set of admissible s is the integer interval [d, H−d]. A non-empty integer interval requires d ≤ H−d. □

Corollary B.2 (latency form). With d = ⌈L_q/Δt⌉ and integer n = ⌊H/2⌋,

L_q ≤ ⌊H/2⌋·Δt = A/2.   (B.2)

Proof. For integer n, ⌈x⌉ ≤ n ⟺ x ≤ n. Setting n = ⌊H/2⌋ gives the result. The conservative design rounds d up, so (B.2) is a sufficient and necessary condition under the rounded convention. □

Corollary B.3 (real-time factor). Writing the generation rate as the real-time factor

R = A/L,   (B.3)

condition (B.2) is equivalent to R ≥ 2.

Proof. A/2 ≥ L ⟺ A/L ≥ 2. □

Note the asymmetry with the weaker "continuous motion" condition R ≥ 1 used by some vendor material: R ≥ 1 says the next chunk is ready before the current one ends, which is necessary for uninterrupted motion but does not guarantee overlap with the committed prefix. T1 requires the stronger R ≥ 2.

B.3 Violation probability and its reporting

Proposition B.4. Under independent chunk generation, the per-chunk probability of a continuity violation is

p_v = 1 − F_L(A/2).   (B.4)

Definition B.5 (success-versus-latency curve). For KT1 we report not only F_L but

S(λ) = Pr[no continuity violation | L = λ],   (B.5)

estimated by replay at injected latencies λ over a pre-registered grid, and the latency λ* at which S(λ*) = 1 − ε for the pre-registered ε. The tail test (B.2) is a pass/fail on λ = A/2; (B.5) is the diagnostic that prevents an arbitrary margin from discarding a viable link (KT1 failure consequence in §6.2).

