Appendix C — Queueing, admission, and EDF feasibility (T4)

C.1 The mean bound and why it is only a bound

Treat the GPU as a single server, deterministic service S, N robots each releasing a request every sΔt seconds independently: an M/D/1 queue with

λ = N/(sΔt),  ρ = λS,  W_q = ρS / (2(1−ρ)).   (C.1)

Limitation C.1. (C.1) assumes Poisson arrivals, fixed S, and a single workload. Three violations are structural, not incidental: synchronized releases across robots; batch-dependent S under continuous batching; and HBM contention between co-resident VLA and critic. (C.1) is retained as a first bound, and KT4 tests it under bursty and synchronized arrival explicitly.

C.2 A hard bound under the admission controller

Definition C.2 (dispatch-time admission). A request i is admitted only if, at the moment it reaches the head of the EDF order,

(k_i + 1)·S ≤ D_i   and   |Q| < Q_max,   (C.2)

where k_i is the number of queued requests with deadline slack D_j ≤ D_i. Otherwise the request is refused (K5).

Proposition C.3 (deadline guarantee). Under Definition C.2 and EDF service order, every admitted request completes before its deadline.

Proof. Requests are served in non-decreasing D order. Request i can be preceded only by the k_i requests with D_j ≤ D_i that are queued at dispatch; each takes at most S. Hence the worst-case completion time of i relative to its dispatch is at most (k_i + 1)S ≤ D_i by (C.2). Refused requests never occupy the queue, so they cannot displace admitted ones. □

Corollary C.4 (queue bound). Since |Q| < Q_max is checked at admission, |Q| ≤ Q_max − 1 is an invariant, and the maximum achievable queueing delay is

W_max = (Q_max − 1)·S.   (C.3)

Corollary C.5 (why Q_max must be swept). (C.3) shows Q_max trades tail delay against refusal rate, with no interior optimum derivable in closed form — the optimum depends on the arrival process, which is measured, not assumed. Hence Q_max is set from the knee of the measured p99-versus-Q_max curve in KT4, and no fixed interval [8, 12] is asserted.

C.3 The full feasibility condition

Collecting the pieces, a configuration (N, s, Q_max, critic context length) is admissible for a given robot only if

(i) N < sΔt/S,
(ii) (Q_max − 1)S + t_rest,i ≤ ⌊H/2⌋Δt for every i,
(iii) M_weights + M_KV + M_act + M_fw ≤ 0.90 M_GPU,
(iv) the refusal rate at the chosen Q_max is below the pre-registered ε_ref,
(v) the EDF dispatch test (C.2) is satisfied for every admitted request.

Remark C.6. (ii) is strictly stronger than the corresponding condition in v2 and v3, because it uses the hard queue bound (C.3) rather than a mean. For S = 27.9 ms [M, RTX Pro 6000], Q_max = 8, t_rest = 40 ms [T], H/2·Δt = 500 ms: the left side is 235 ms, leaving 265 ms of headroom — admissible. For Q_max = 12: 347 ms, leaving 153 ms — still admissible but with a refusal margin that KT4 must quantify.

C.4 Critic starvation

Definition C.7. Under strict VLA-over-critic class priority, the critic starvation fraction over a window T is

σ(T) = 1 − (service time delivered to the critic in T)/(critic demand in T).   (C.4)

Remark C.8. Two policies are admissible and must be chosen explicitly, not left implicit: a reserved-slot policy guaranteeing a minimum critic rate r_min (e.g. 1 Hz), or a best-effort policy with σ measured and reported. The dissertation adopts whichever KT3 shows to be implementable, and states which.

