Appendix D — The three capture-region approximations and their gap

D.1 LIPM analytic approximation

For the linear inverted pendulum with CoM height z and ω = √(g/z), the capture point is

ξ = x + ẋ/ω,   (D.1)

and the capture region for the LIPM with a flywheel admits an exact closed-form description (Pratt et al., 2006). With z = 0.9 m, ω = 3.30 s⁻¹ and 1/ω ≈ 303 ms [C].

Approximation D.1. ℛ_cap^LIPM ⊇ ℛ_cap in general, because it neglects joint limits, torque saturation, contact friction cones, and multi-contact transitions. It is therefore conservative for placement, and its conservatism is the reason KT6 can pass under it while the true robot is less capable — or fail under it while the true robot is more capable.

D.2 N-step Monte Carlo capturable set

Definition D.2. ℛ_cap^N(φ) is the empirical set of states from which a nominal controller reaches a capture point within N steps, estimated by Monte Carlo over the state distribution and contacts, with a stated sample size and confidence interval.

Remark D.3. KT6 as specified in v2 and v3 risked circularity: testing a simulator's balance controller against the same simulator's capturable set measures model self-consistency, not hardware feasibility. The three-approximation scheme is designed to break that circularity by reporting the disagreement rather than a single pass/fail.

D.3 Hardware fall indicator

Definition D.4. ℛ_cap^HW is the binary indicator obtained from tethered-rig and simulation fall trials in KT5a2: a state is capturable in the hardware sense iff the fallback policy reaches a balanced standstill or controlled fall from it within the pre-registered trial count.

D.5 The approximation gap

Definition D.5. With |·| denoting measure under the state sampling distribution used in KT6,

δ_cap = |ℛ_cap^LIPM Δ ℛ_cap^N| / |ℛ_cap^LIPM ∪ ℛ_cap^N|,   (D.2)

the Jaccard distance between the conservative analytic region and the empirical region.

Reporting rule D.6. KT6 reports the capture-region exit probability under all three approximations plus δ_cap. A placement is confirmed if the inequality of §3.4 holds under ℛ_cap^LIPM and is not contradicted under ℛ_cap^N; it is unresolved if the three disagree beyond a pre-registered δ_cap^max; it is rejected if it fails under ℛ_cap^LIPM. No placement is justified by ℛ_cap^N alone.

