Appendix H — B0's deterministic decision rule

This appendix writes out the rule that §7.2 asserts, so that "deterministic" is a claim about code, not about intent. It is a specification, not an implementation, and it has not been executed.

```
B0.tick(proprioception x, heartbeat hb, chunk_buffer B2):

  # 1. Limits: always, unconditionally, no learned component.
  x_safe = clamp_to_joint_torque_stability_limits(x)

  # 2. Heartbeat and detection.
  if age(hb) > T_det_max:                      # T_det_max from KT9
      return constrained_stop(x_safe)          # deterministic

  # 3. Corner case: B2 refused AND margin low.
  if B2.last_decision == REFUSE:
      m = margin_to_capture_boundary(x_safe)   # deterministic geometry
      if m < m_d:                              # m_d pre-registered, KT5a2
          return damping_fallback(x_safe)      # deterministic

  # 4. Normal path.
  if B2.last_decision == ACCEPT and not expired(B2.head):
      return track(B2.head)
  else:
      return constrained_stop(x_safe)          # deterministic

  # Estimator output is logged, never read by this function.
  # log(estimator(x)) is written by a separate, non-actuating task.
```

Properties asserted by construction, to be verified by code audit and KT5a2:

· P1 (no learned component on the path). No branch condition reads estimator(·). The estimator's output appears only in the log.
· P2 (total function). Every combination of (limits, heartbeat, B2 decision, expiry) maps to exactly one of {track, constrained_stop, damping_fallback}. No state is unhandled.
· P3 (monotone degradation). The three outputs are ordered by authority: track ⊑ constrained_stop ⊑ damping_fallback, and the rule never returns a weaker output under a stronger failure signal.
· P4 (time-bounded). Each branch's worst-case execution time is bounded and independent of the link, since margin_to_capture_boundary is a geometric computation over proprioception only.

Remark H.1. P1 is the formal content of the claim "the estimator is telemetry only." If any future revision allows the estimator to select among branches, P1 fails, the estimator moves onto the certification path, and T2b must be widened to include it. This is a scope limit, not a design preference.

