Appendix K — Scope limits, open problems, and what this document is not

K.1 Scope limits (stated, not closed)

1. T2b — the certification gap. No governing standard specifies a humanoid's active safe-stop chain; the robot-side reaction chain establishes no PFHD (Ding et al.). "Safe independent of the link" is a design goal, not a certified claim.
2. MCS7 — semantic chunk errors. A chunk can pass the error tube and the capture-region check and still be semantically wrong. B2 cannot catch it; the residual is carried by an operator-in-the-loop safeguard (KT10).
3. Statistical rather than certifying evidence. Every test in §6.2 is bounded at the ~1% level by 299 trials. None certifies a hazard rate. Corollary 3.2 shows why a certifying sample is out of reach within a project of this size.
4. Third-party hardware. Every [M] figure describes someone else's machine. The dissertation's own measurements begin at Phase 0.5.

K.2 Open problems this document does not attempt

· A certifiable formulation of the SDA-to-balancing-policy interface, including a fault-behaviour specification and a DC argument.
· A method for certifying a learned stoppability estimator, as opposed to validating it at the performance level.
· A within-chunk contact-feasibility guarantee that is provable rather than learned; the 2026 correctors are empirical.
· A quantitative risk model for the link, which would require either (1) or a sample size beyond Corollary 3.2.
· Non-industrial scope for ISO 25785-1, which the draft does not cover even after publication.

K.3 What this document is not

It is not a report of experiments. It is not a certification argument. It is not a claim that the workstation is 6.5× Thor for the workload that matters — it is 2× single-stream and the argument rests on batched, prefill-heavy work. It is not a claim that the body is safe; it is a claim that the body is designed so that the link is outside the safety argument, and that this design is testable.

It is a pre-registration. The next artefact is the Phase 0.5 record, and the cheapest decisive falsification — the real-time factor R of a chunk-generating policy on the 5090 at the TensorRT recipe — sits at its front, where it can be measured before a robot is bought. 🌹🧠

